#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1140.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120913} :activity/id #uuid "7247ca88-471f-5303-aa77-7e868bf58346" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048377 :topic/id #uuid "9a5bcec0-860b-5cc9-83b6-331b8b7e1d13" :topic/math-academy-id 1792} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120913 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121135 :tutorial/content "Consider the curve $C$ parametrized by the vector-valued function \n\n$$\n\\mathbf f(t) = \\langle t+1, \\: t^2 +1\\rangle, \\qquad t \\in (-\\infty, \\infty).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this function, we get\n\n$$\n\\mathbf f'(t) = \\langle 1, \\: 2t\\rangle.\n$$\n\nEvaluating this function and its derivative at $t=0,$ we get the following pair of vectors:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf f(0)  =  \\left\\langle 1, 1 \\right\\rangle, \\qquad \\mathbf f'(0)  =  \\left\\langle 1, 0 \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nIf we view $\\mathbf{f}'(0)$ as a vector attached to the tip of the position vector $\\mathbf f(0),$ then it will be tangent to the curve $C,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/85/854d16c788836f784d739f73063354fa3ca2ad233f03cce53b99e5764449f585.png)\n\nSince $\\mathbf f'(0) \\neq \\mathbf{0},$ it serves as a direction vector for the tangent line to the curve at $\\mathbf f(0).$ The equation of this line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(u) & =f(0)+u{f}^{′}(0) \\\\  & =⟨1,1⟩+u⟨1,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1+u,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $u \\in (-\\infty, \\infty).$\n\nIn general, if a curve $C$ is parametrized by a vector function $\\mathbf f(t),$ then the vector $\\mathbf f'(t)$ gives a **tangent vector** to $C$ at $\\mathbf{f} (t),$ provided that $\\mathbf f'(t)\\neq \\mathbf{0}.$\n\nFor a fixed $t$ in the domain of $\\mathbf{f}(t),$ the equation of the corresponding tangent line is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{R}(u) = \\mathbf{f}(t) + u\\mathbf{f}'(t), \\qquad u \\in (-\\infty, \\infty).\n$$\n\nFurthermore, the vector $\\mathbf f'(t)$ always points in the direction that the curve $C$ is oriented. In our example above, as $t$ increases and passes through the point $(1,1),$ the curve is transversed from left to right, and the direction of the vector $\\mathbf{f}'(0)$ is also from left to right." :tutorial/id #uuid "dbd32d96-f4fc-5f66-861c-fc1b9746be10" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7864 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "2d2f798c-9ffb-57a0-aea4-4212795c1dcc" :step/math-academy-id 45344 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120914}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120914 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119943 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119513 :question/id #uuid "7d93dd10-5319-58e7-9106-f8c675dc9e65" :question/math-academy-id "e-12567"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9c2fbd5-33cf-51fb-b95d-cf5277f3e4ae" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048999}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120212} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120214} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262642} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262643} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262644} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262645} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345043} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345044} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345045} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503739}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent Vector"} :step/id #uuid "989791d3-66ad-598a-a711-f5c1c0da6f59" :step/math-academy-id 45345 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120915}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120915 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119944 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119514 :question/id #uuid "42109de0-eb59-53f9-982e-1433f630b067" :question/math-academy-id "e-12568"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "60e28b26-0eb7-51ed-bcf9-2660b98a6028" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049485}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120108} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120215} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262656} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262657} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304794} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308635} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308643} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308650} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308704} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345046} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363010} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400240} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400247} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405125} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433175}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent Line to a Curve"} :step/id #uuid "e0c03cce-2d21-571e-98fe-f72a42288e4a" :step/math-academy-id 45348 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120917}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120916 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119945 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119515 :question/id #uuid "26c3579b-5b24-5e84-9024-5b2597856d20" :question/math-academy-id "e-12569"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4636870-89db-5bf9-8a04-777407957e33" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049715}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120109} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120216} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262664} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284532} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284533} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284534} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284535} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284536} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308725} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311545} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363527} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398604} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410824} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503676} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503679} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503699} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503747}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points Where a Tangent Vector is Parallel to Another Vector"} :step/id #uuid "74ac7b94-509d-54d2-9176-80fb0b50e930" :step/math-academy-id 45351} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120917 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119946 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119516 :question/id #uuid "e8c24f26-7658-5251-922a-4b34043a71d4" :question/math-academy-id "e-12580"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "297a4812-a87a-592a-818a-8c8d3d3f7868" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048954}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120253} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120522} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262662} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262663} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284525} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284526} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284527} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284528} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284529} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345047} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345102} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345103} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503725}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points Where a Tangent Vector is Perpendicular to Another Vector"} :step/id #uuid "ad5a98da-8ea4-5205-9db2-94f0f8b668ab" :step/math-academy-id 45392 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120916}}] :activity/title "Tangent Vectors and Tangent Lines to Curves" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186120011}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410826} :activity/id #uuid "27a4662b-38f4-5da4-a14f-0ae0dbfbda48" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048377 :topic/id #uuid "9a5bcec0-860b-5cc9-83b6-331b8b7e1d13" :topic/math-academy-id 1792} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410825 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410824 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38f7dbd3-9ac6-59ba-822d-baee2e1a48d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(3,-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410818} {:answer/id #uuid "4ad7606a-78cf-5b5a-aad3-516d0783136b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-1,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410819} {:answer/id #uuid "6e2a15f9-1731-51c6-a7aa-e028d3d2c84e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(0,7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410820} {:answer/id #uuid "b2e66482-ad5e-56f0-ab9c-0187ad900545" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(0,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410821} {:answer/id #uuid "c15d36f2-d954-5b67-a997-5ab2ddb7afaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(8,-9)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410822}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410818} :answer-field/id #uuid "236f2410-bb87-524a-b10a-c030da587949" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410823}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "eb19afc5-d677-5f22-bac0-92428e650b61" :question/math-academy-id "q-126900" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨{t}^{2}-2t,4t-1⟩,$ find the point at which the tangent to the curve is parallel to the vector $⟨-1,1⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t-2,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $⟨-1\\,,\\,1⟩$ are parallel for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that ${r}^{′}({t}_{0})=c⟨-1,1⟩.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =c⟨-1,1⟩ \\\\ ⟨2t-2,4⟩ & =c⟨-1,1⟩ \\\\ ⟨2t-2,4⟩ & =⟨-c,c⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the components gives:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2t-2=-c \\\\ 4=c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we obtain that $c=4.$ Substituting $c=4$ into the first equation, we get $t=-1.$ \n\nTherefore, $⟨-1,1⟩$ and ${r}^{′}$ are parallel only at the point corresponding to $t=-1.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(-1) & =⟨(-1)^{2}-2(-1),4(-1)-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,-5⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $⟨-1,1⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are parallel at the point $(3,-5).$"} :step/id #uuid "25cc0c4b-5364-577c-92a1-008375ee5f34" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410828}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410826 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345046 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3bdb4010-1e08-5378-a110-856c1b0d31e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨0,\\,1,\\,1⟩+u⟨1,\\,1,\\,-1⟩,\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342732} {:answer/id #uuid "8844755e-17ca-5997-8087-0445418bac62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨0,\\,1,\\,1⟩+u⟨-1,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩,\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343418} {:answer/id #uuid "97e8fdd1-1609-5108-82df-e53e59510619" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨0,\\,1,1⟩+u⟨1,\\,0,\\,1⟩,\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343570} {:answer/id #uuid "d3b6012c-303d-5b23-b10c-010c9c93ca49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨0,\\,1,1⟩+u⟨1,\\,-1,0⟩,\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344097} {:answer/id #uuid "fd335b51-418d-5e75-99ad-992ab400d4c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨0,\\,1,\\,1⟩+u⟨0,\\,-1,\\,1⟩,\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344464}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342732} :answer-field/id #uuid "c171024e-4b6d-5601-9cbd-861fac1471b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344833}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a928b0be-2c4b-5a25-9350-6790b8cc0365" :question/math-academy-id "q-75203" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the equation of the tangent to the curve $r(t)=⟨t,\\,{e}^{t},\\,{e}^{-t}⟩$ at the point where $t=0,$  valid for all  real values of $u?$" :question/worked-solution "At the point where $t=0$,  the vector ${r}^{′}(0)$ is tangent to the curve, so the tangent line to the curve at $P$ can be parametrized as\n\n \n\n$$\nR(u)=r(0)+u{r}^{′}(0),\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we find $r(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,\\,{e}^{0},\\,{e}^{-0}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n   \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{-t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,{e}^{t},\\,-{e}^{-t}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate ${r}^{′}(t)$ at $t=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(0) & =⟨1,\\,{e}^{0},\\,-{e}^{-0}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,1,\\,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the given curve at the point \n where $t=0$ is\n\n$$\nR(u)=⟨0,\\,1,\\,1⟩+u⟨1,\\,1,\\,-1⟩,\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "588c5c9a-8d3d-51b8-8b90-dff365fed144" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410825}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410827 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284525 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a91054f-adf7-5f56-83c2-fa3119dc478c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{16},-\\frac{7}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282606} {:answer/id #uuid "88ea9708-0042-5422-b8c9-124b38c78848" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(64,2)$ and $(\\frac{1}{16},-\\frac{7}{4})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282871} {:answer/id #uuid "cbc619de-3849-50d7-a032-ed5d0fa3212b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(64,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283469} {:answer/id #uuid "dc37b06f-ca47-55ac-9f76-e24057ce58fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(2\\,,\\,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283609} {:answer/id #uuid "f38a4e4e-a50c-5a8c-9c70-c08d603abc61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(2,-1)$ and $(\\frac{1}{16},-\\frac{7}{4})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283818}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283609} :answer-field/id #uuid "109ae18e-4733-5298-895a-59e0b555d56b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283966}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "854e79dd-e49f-5d57-896b-a1669e5ffce7" :question/math-academy-id "q-164833" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨2{t}^{5}\\,,\\,{t}^{2}-2⟩,$ $t\\in (0,\\infty ),$ find all of the points at which the tangent to the curve is perpendicular to the vector $⟨1\\,,\\,-5⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{5})\\,,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨10{t}^{4}\\,,\\,2t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors  $⟨1\\,,\\,-5⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular for some value of the parameter $t$ if and only if $⟨1\\,,\\,-5⟩\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨1\\,,\\,-5⟩\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨1\\,,\\,-5⟩\\cdot ⟨10{t}^{4}\\,,\\,2t⟩ & =0 \\\\ 1\\cdot 10{t}^{4}+(-5)\\cdot 2t & =0 \\\\ 10{t}^{4}-10t & =0 \\\\ 10t({t}^{3}-1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t\\in (0,\\infty )$, the only solution is $t=1.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =⟨2(1)^{5}\\,,\\,(1)^{2}-2⟩=⟨2\\,,\\,-1⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n we conclude that $⟨1\\,,\\,-5⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular at the point $(2\\,,\\,-1).$"} :step/id #uuid "677b5852-f95c-533d-b31c-e87dce9ad2d7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410829}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410828 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120109 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8311c425-96db-50b4-bda2-7afc21298e5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(0,5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261013} {:answer/id #uuid "970d51ce-6cc7-567e-b921-0a5f575739b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(10,-7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261184} {:answer/id #uuid "aed5ed6e-37c8-5e3c-ae55-c95e30ce3202" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(6,-7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261416} {:answer/id #uuid "bc52c38c-023f-560d-827e-ce988bae6a2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(1,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261533} {:answer/id #uuid "c912ae60-77c2-546c-be59-6f64006178e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(3,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261652}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261533} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c61931c-b6de-57e6-b862-b7df723296cc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262146}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "22d6e6f4-daf0-5645-a375-f0c335545e70" :question/math-academy-id "q-126902" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨2{t}^{2}-t,5-3{t}^{2}⟩,$ find the point at which the tangent to the curve is parallel to the vector $⟨-1,2⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2}-t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5-3{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4t-1,-6t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $⟨-1\\,,\\,2⟩$ are parallel for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that ${r}^{′}({t}_{0})=c⟨-1,2⟩.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =c⟨-1,2⟩ \\\\ ⟨4t-1,-6t⟩ & =c⟨-1,2⟩ \\\\ ⟨4t-1,-6t⟩ & =⟨-c,2c⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the components gives:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4t-1=-c \\\\ -6t=2c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain that $c=-4t+1.$ Substituting $c=-4t+1$ into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6t & =2(-4t+1) \\\\ -6t & =-8t+2 \\\\ 2t & =2 \\\\ t & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $⟨-1,2⟩$ and ${r}^{′}$ are parallel only at the point corresponding to $t=1.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =⟨2(1)^{2}-1,5-3(1)^{2}⟩=⟨1,2⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $⟨-1,2⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are parallel at the point $(1,2).$"} :step/id #uuid "881f99cb-89c3-507b-a7a0-8ef265378f7b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410827}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410829 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284532 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "580c3843-6361-5a93-8e93-aa4eecc3f021" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(24,26)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282438} {:answer/id #uuid "95ec2ae6-b848-5b2c-9e73-71480e6476c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-9,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282983} {:answer/id #uuid "9978da76-99f6-51aa-835d-d6470ba9b397" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-7,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283023} {:answer/id #uuid "c39a49da-d2c5-5e18-8270-20d823e49792" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-9,-4)$ and $(-8,-6)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283395} {:answer/id #uuid "ca04d778-71e9-52fd-ba77-a58b5aaf9a03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-7,-4)$ and $(24,26)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283453}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282438} :answer-field/id #uuid "543918ac-60ea-556f-87e9-9fe723e51c77" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284072}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5a127c57-e392-5180-8b85-86041bead0c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-165038" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨{t}^{5}-8,2{t}^{4}-6⟩,$ $t\\in (0,\\infty )$, find the point at which the tangent to the curve is parallel to the vector $⟨1,\\frac{4}{5}⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{5}-8),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{4}-6)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5{t}^{4},8{t}^{3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $⟨1,\\frac{4}{5}⟩$ are parallel for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that ${r}^{′}({t}_{0})=c⟨1,\\frac{4}{5}⟩.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =c⟨1,\\frac{4}{5}⟩ \\\\ ⟨5{t}^{4},8{t}^{3}⟩ & =c⟨1,\\frac{4}{5}⟩ \\\\ ⟨5{t}^{4},8{t}^{3}⟩ & =⟨c,\\frac{4c}{5}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the components gives:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}5{t}^{4}=c \\\\ 8{t}^{3}=\\frac{4c}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain that $c=5{t}^{4}.$ Substituting $c=5{t}^{4}$ into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{t}^{3} & =\\frac{4(5{t}^{4})}{5} \\\\ 8{t}^{3}-4{t}^{4} & =0 \\\\ 4{t}^{3}(2-t) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t\\in (0,\\infty )$, the only solution is $t=2.$ \n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(2) & =⟨(2)^{5}-8,2(2)^{4}-6⟩=⟨24,26⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $⟨1,\\frac{4}{5}⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are parallel at the point $(24,26).$"} :step/id #uuid "c80e8392-766a-5d4f-9dc1-c5ef5cb2463f"}] :activity/title "Tangent Vectors and Tangent Lines to Curves" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410817}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510544} :activity/id #uuid "4ac98174-23fb-56c7-aa42-87bd53136ffc" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048377 :topic/id #uuid "9a5bcec0-860b-5cc9-83b6-331b8b7e1d13" :topic/math-academy-id 1792} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510496 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262656 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "73ad5d7c-1e63-5d51-ab12-788e64efefe3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨3,-1⟩+u⟨3,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260886} {:answer/id #uuid "8cc3da8e-f83a-5c01-b07d-536ada58e922" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨5,1⟩+u⟨3,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261105} {:answer/id #uuid "ac417ca0-c729-53cd-8682-3380b0ae03c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨3,-1⟩+u⟨1,5⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261382} {:answer/id #uuid "afe3db2a-04c2-508c-bc89-313d4a9d15a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨5,1⟩+u⟨3,-3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261431} {:answer/id #uuid "c438a2ee-3595-508d-8254-e138020e7fde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨5,1⟩+u⟨1,5⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261607}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261105} :answer-field/id #uuid "5fa8cbcb-e3fc-5a33-b19e-07f1620df0e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262287}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "98889633-5319-50f9-95bf-ebaeba1b9d31" :question/math-academy-id "q-126835" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the equation of the tangent to the curve $r(t)=⟨3t+5,1-{t}^{3}⟩$ at the point where $t=0,$  valid for all  real values of $u?$" :question/worked-solution "At the point where $t=0,$ the vector ${r}^{′}(0)$ is tangent to the curve, so the tangent line to the curve at $P$ can be parametrized as\n \n\n$$\nR(u)=r(0)+u{r}^{′}(0),\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we find $r(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨3(0)+5,1-{0}^{3}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n   \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t+5),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-{t}^{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,-3{t}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate ${r}^{′}(t)$ at $t=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(0) & =⟨3,-3(0)^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the given curve at the point where $t=0$ is\n\n$$\nR(u)=⟨5,1⟩+u⟨3,0⟩,\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "3ffa00d6-c195-5c51-ba40-87eda1859e72" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510525}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510525 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262664 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03f4eddd-9c61-5352-a69a-1571210da725" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-5,-1)$ and $(\\frac{20}{9},\\frac{8}{27})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259911} {:answer/id #uuid "11fe2bb9-1108-5d28-b12c-8890cff2d165" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(\\frac{4}{3},\\frac{5}{9})$  and $(0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260032} {:answer/id #uuid "5e5624d4-89b1-542b-b638-adfb8d2a014e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-5,-1)$ and $(0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260684} {:answer/id #uuid "fa38c570-2ffe-5805-b302-902cb4c665be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(0,0)$ and $(\\frac{20}{9},\\frac{8}{27})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262061} {:answer/id #uuid "fd77e1df-98bd-5a78-a540-748adbdc49c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(3,1)$ and $(\\frac{4}{3},\\frac{5}{9})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262096}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259911} :answer-field/id #uuid "7308447e-ddce-597a-891f-38f71ec1133a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262317}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "15bef614-c58b-5a73-8f54-ec8a2bc3ef51" :question/math-academy-id "q-126901" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨4t-{t}^{2},{t}^{3}⟩,$ find all points at which the tangent to the curve is parallel to the vector $⟨2,1⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t-{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4-2t,3{t}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $⟨0\\,,\\,1\\,,\\,3⟩$ are parallel for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that ${r}^{′}({t}_{0})=c⟨2,1⟩.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =c⟨2,1⟩ \\\\ ⟨4-2t,3{t}^{2}⟩ & =c⟨2,1⟩ \\\\ ⟨4-2t,3{t}^{2}⟩ & =⟨2c,c⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the components gives:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4-2t=2c \\\\ 3{t}^{2}=c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain that $c=2-t.$ Substituting in the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{t}^{2} & =2-t \\\\ 3{t}^{2}+t-2 & =0 \\\\ (3t-2)(t+1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $⟨2,1⟩$ and ${r}^{′}$ are parallel only at the points corresponding to $t=-1,\\frac{2}{3}.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(-1) & =⟨4(-1)-(-1)^{2},(-1)^{3}⟩=⟨-5,-1⟩ \\\\ r(\\frac{2}{3}) & =⟨4(\\frac{2}{3})-{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{2},{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{3}⟩=⟨\\frac{20}{9},\\frac{8}{27}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $⟨2,1⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are parallel at the points $(-5,-1)$ and $(\\frac{20}{9},\\frac{8}{27}).$"} :step/id #uuid "9a79291f-1a57-5bc2-94db-68fc1780e073" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510566}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510527 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345102 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "70c234f6-092e-5f46-865a-425945404213" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-1,0)$ and $(1,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343206} {:answer/id #uuid "72377fdc-c5c4-5cad-be35-e357b4b262ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(1,1)$ and $(-1,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343227} {:answer/id #uuid "8be39f08-c60d-56cb-867b-ae591af34e37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-1,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343461} {:answer/id #uuid "9f960098-4e3f-597c-a84f-8d0dfb1daa4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-1,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343646} {:answer/id #uuid "ee8ce6f6-003a-5a0f-82e6-f33b9254bcdd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(1,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344335}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343227} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1ca2441-2869-5b83-a4db-5247f47eb190" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344834}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "2a9495b4-c709-53eb-875a-846142be09cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-75521" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=t\\,i+\\frac{1}{t}\\,j$, find all of the points at which ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are perpendicular." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{t})\\,j \\\\  & =i-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular for some value of the parameter $t$ if and only if $r(t)\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ [t\\,i+\\frac{1}{t}\\,j]\\cdot (i-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\,j) & =0 \\\\ t\\cdot 1-\\frac{1}{t}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{t}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ t-\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}} & =0 \\\\ {t}^{4} & =1 \\\\ t & =\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $t=\\pm 1.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1)=1\\,i+\\frac{1}{1}\\,j=\\,i+j, \\\\ r(-1)=(-1)\\,i+\\frac{1}{(-1)}\\,j=-\\,i-j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n we conclude that $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular at the points $(1,1)$ and $(-1,-1).$"} :step/id #uuid "9feee794-e3e7-5c76-b0a7-b75fa9c75d71" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510496}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510544 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405125 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "109c8716-781f-5d87-93da-32c8a82a52af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-i-\\frac{3}{2}\\,k+u(\\pi \\,i+\\,k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405127} {:answer/id #uuid "2a440298-d0fd-5f7c-ade9-88076046ff22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-i+\\frac{3}{2}\\,k+u(\\pi \\,i+\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405128} {:answer/id #uuid "62d667da-2ed7-5305-afe2-d228189c5402" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-i+\\frac{3}{2}\\,k+u(\\pi \\,j+\\,k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405129} {:answer/id #uuid "6b671d99-5618-53cb-8be2-7e346528c73a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-i+\\frac{3}{2}\\,k+u(-\\pi \\,j-\\,k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405130} {:answer/id #uuid "e549fd1f-32f0-5d47-bc19-3d8ea851a377" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-i+\\frac{3}{2}\\,k+u(i+\\pi \\,j+\\,k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405131}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405129} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc1f347e-fe06-5ef7-a082-5d1425a121dd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405126}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "33bfb8ae-51e5-575a-aee2-202f3434e957" :question/math-academy-id "q-32876" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the equation of the tangent to the curve $r(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t\\,j+t\\,k$ at the point $P(-1,0,\\frac{3}{2}),$ parametrized for real values of $u$ so that increasing $u$ follows the direction of increasing $t$?" :question/worked-solution "The point $P(-1,0,\\frac{3}{2})$ is at the tip of $r(t)$ for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t.$ At this point,  the vector ${r}^{′}({t}_{0})$ is tangent to the curve, so the tangent line to the curve at $P$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nR(u)=r({t}_{0})+u\\,{r}^{′}({t}_{0}),\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to $P.$ We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =-i+0\\,j+\\frac{3}{2}\\,k \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t\\,j+t\\,k & =-i+0\\,j+\\frac{3}{2}\\,k,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand equating the coefficients of $i,j,$ and $k$ yields the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t=-1 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t=0 \\\\ t=\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get $t=\\frac{3}{2}.$ This value also satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, $P(-1,0,\\frac{3}{2})$ is at the tip of the vector $r(\\frac{3}{2}).$  \n\nWe calculate ${r}^{′}(t):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,k \\\\  & =\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t\\,i-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t\\,j+\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate ${r}^{′}(t)$ at $t=\\frac{3}{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(\\frac{3}{2}) & =\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})\\,i-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})\\,j+\\,k \\\\  & =0\\,i+\\pi \\,j+\\,k \\\\  & =\\pi \\,j+\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the given curve at the point $P(-1,0,\\frac{3}{2})$ is\n\n$$\nR(u)=-i+\\frac{3}{2}\\,k+u\\,(\\pi \\,j+\\,k),\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "ccf8c39a-29e3-592d-8a48-1ca1d41e9f28" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510527}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510566 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284535 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05b526ee-e645-545d-9d38-eba3bf488f13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(e,\\frac{5}{4})$ and $({e}^{-2},\\frac{1}{2})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281738} {:answer/id #uuid "14e0f6aa-69fa-5a2a-a5a2-05425a8ac3c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(1,\\frac{1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281883} {:answer/id #uuid "5c2c1ef9-8c20-5ce3-95b9-c866ecb62eb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "({e}^{2},\\frac{5}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282469} {:answer/id #uuid "944b8abb-39ab-55bb-808e-d4b88ed9adfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(1,\\frac{1}{2})$ and $({e}^{2},\\frac{5}{2})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282963} {:answer/id #uuid "bb28ac5d-2a0c-5e78-8e08-1b94f7e7ed42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(e,\\frac{5}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283305}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281883} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0b7424d-a230-5a57-a288-0f3d6f9aa64c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284358}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "c1d5d096-073b-5c49-80fa-a124c3af6414" :question/math-academy-id "q-165042" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)={e}^{2t}\\,i+({t}^{2}+t+\\frac{1}{2})\\,j$, find all points at which ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are parallel." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{2t})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+t+\\frac{1}{2})\\,j \\\\  & =2{e}^{2t}\\,i+(2t+1)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are parallel for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that $r({t}_{0})=c\\,{r}^{′}({t}_{0}).$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =c\\,{r}^{′}(t) \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\,i+({t}^{2}+t+\\frac{1}{2})\\,j & =c(2{e}^{2t}\\,i+(2t+1)\\,j) \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\,i+({t}^{2}+t+\\frac{1}{2})\\,j & =2c{e}^{2t}\\,i+c(2t+1)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $i$ and $j$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t}=2c{e}^{2t} \\\\ {t}^{2}+t+\\frac{1}{2}=c(2t+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain $c=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Substituting $c=\\frac{1}{2}$ into the second equality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}+t+\\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(2t+1) \\\\ {t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ t & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $r$ and ${r}^{′}$ are parallel at the point corresponding to $t=0.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & ={e}^{0}\\,i+({0}^{2}+0+\\frac{1}{2})\\,j \\\\  & =i+\\frac{1}{2}\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are parallel at the point $(1,\\frac{1}{2}).$"} :step/id #uuid "feabdf5c-4763-5d3a-872a-568b04e425bd"}] :activity/title "Tangent Vectors and Tangent Lines to Curves" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509683}]}
